<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhodri the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 820–<a href="/years/878">878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Rhodri Mawr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rhodri the Great, Rhodri Mawr, was king of Gwynedd from 844 who also gained Powys and Seisyllwg, ruling most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. He won a notable victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raiders in 856. He was killed fighting the English in 878, and his descendants founded the leading dynasties of medieval Wales.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhodri-the-great.jpg" alt="Rhodri the Great" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Powell · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhodri_Mawr_-_Historiae_Cambriae.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rhodri ap Merfyn was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merfyn-frych">Merfyn Frych</a>, king of Gwynedd, and Nest, daughter of Cadell ap Brochfael of Powys. Merfyn set out from his native Isle of Man, probably with Norse help, and made his bid for the throne of Gwynedd in 826 as a representative of the line of Cynan Dindaethwy, whose only child Esyllt was Merfyn's mother. Merfyn died in 844 and Rhodri succeeded him. He appears to have kept his father's rule in both Gwynedd and the Isle of Man, since a contemporary poem on his death in the margin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-ulster">Annals of Ulster</a> calls him Ruaidri of Man.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Few events of the reign are known. In 853 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgred-of-mercia">Burgred of Mercia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelwulf-of-wessex">Æthelwulf of Wessex</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and received its submission, and Rhodri may have been among those who submitted. In 856 he killed Orm, a leader of the viking faction that Irish sources call the Dubgenti, and this may be the victory marked by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poem by Sedulius Scottus. In 877 the Dubgenti attacked him in the Sunday Battle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a> and forced him to take refuge in Ireland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rhodri returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in 878 and was killed, together with his son Gwriad, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceolwulf-ii-of-mercia">Ceolwulf II of Mercia</a>. Three years later the Annales Cambriæ record a Battle of the Conwy, as revenge for Rhodri, fought by his sons against Ceolwulf's successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-of-mercia">Æthelred of Mercia</a>. After Mercian overlordship was broken, his sons Anarawd and Cadell overran all of Wales except the south-east, with Anarawd keeping Gwynedd and Cadell holding Ceredigion and later Dyfed. His reign probably saw the codification of the earliest surviving Welsh genealogies, those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty</a> of Gwynedd, the oldest such collection in medieval Welsh literature.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rhodri Mawr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Rhodri the Great</b> killed the Danish leader Horm in 856, and in 881 his son Anarawd defeated the Mercians at Conwy, a victory the annals called vengeance for Rhodri.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Powys is then usually said to have passed to <b>Rhodri the Great</b> of Gwynedd, whose mother Nest is traditionally named as Cyngen’s sister, and for two centuries it was usually governed by kings based outside it, the last and greatest of them Gruffudd ap Llywelyn (d. 1063).</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its last native king, Gwgon, drowned in 871, and it passed by marriage to <b>Rhodri the Great</b> of Gwynedd, whose son Cadell inherited it.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-seisyllwg">Kingdom of Seisyllwg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Isle of Man. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.29,-4.38&amp;year=857" aria-label="Anglesey on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4452 330 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Rhodri the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4902 600h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4902 600h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.29,-4.38&amp;year=857">What was here in 857 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
